Let $n \ge 2$.
\begin{itemize}
\item Given a proper rational map $f: \mathbb{B}^{n} \rightarrow \mathbb{B}^{N}$, find the smallest number $k(n, N)$ such that $f$ is determined by its $k$-jet at the origin.

\item Let $\Omega, \Omega'$ in $\mathbb{C}^{n}$ and $\mathbb{C}^{N}$ respectively be strongly pseudoconvex domains. If $f: \Omega \rightarrow \Omega'$ is a proper holomorphic map which extends smoothly to $\partial \Omega$, does there exist a $k$ such that $f$ is determined by its $k$-jet at a point?
\end{itemize}
